```typescript
export function resolveDefaultModelForAgent(params: {
  cfg: OpenClawConfig;
  agentId?: string;
}): ModelRef {
  const agentModelOverride = params.agentId
    ? resolveAgentEffectiveModelPrimary(params.cfg, params.agentId)
    : undefined;
  const cfg =
    agentModelOverride && agentModelOverride.length > 0
      ? {
          ...params.cfg,
          agents: {
            ...params.cfg.agents,
            defaults: {
              ...params.cfg.agents?.defaults,
              model: {
                ...toAgentModelListLike(params.cfg.agents?.defaults?.model),
                primary: agentModelOverride,
              },
            },
          },
        }
      : params.cfg;
  return resolveConfiguredModelRef({
    cfg,
    defaultProvider: DEFAULT_PROVIDER,
    defaultModel: DEFAULT_MODEL,
  });
}
```

And `resolveConfiguredModelRef`:
```typescript
export function resolveConfiguredModelRef(params: {
  cfg: OpenClawConfig;
  defaultProvider: string;
  defaultModel: string;
}): Model
```

So the function checks if there's an agent-specific model override, applies it to the config if present, then uses that to resolve the default model reference. The key thing is that `resolveConfiguredModelRef` is being called with the potentially modified configuration that now includes the agent's model override. I need to trace through `resolveAgentEffectiveModelPrimary` to see where this is coming from. Let me also look at the actual config file to understand what's happening. Actually, the bug report mentions `openai-codex/gpt-5.4` but the model set was `ollama/nemotron-3-super:cloud` — the new default isn't even in the same provider, so something else is going on here.

