OK! Now I found it! Look at lines 330-338:```typescript
const configuredProviders = params.cfg.models?.providers;
if (configuredProviders && typeof configuredProviders === "object") {
  const hasDefaultProvider = Boolean(configuredProviders[params.defaultProvider]);
  if (!hasDefaultProvider) {    const availableProvider = Object.entries(configuredProviders).find(
      ([, providerCfg]) =>        providerCfg &&
        Array.isArray(providerCfg.models) &&
        providerCfg.models.length > 0 &&
        providerCfg.models[0]?.id,
    );
    if (availableProvider) {
      const [providerName, providerCfg] = availableProvider;
      const firstModel = providerCfg.models[0];
      return { provider: providerName, model: firstModel.id };
    }
  }
}
```

When the configured default provider isn't available, the code searches through the available providers and returns the first one found, along with its first model's ID. But wait—the issue title says "Gateway default model 在 `openclaw models set` + service restart 后未更新", so the actual problem is that the model isn't updating after restart, not that it's falling back to a default.

Let me think through the flow more carefully. After `models set ollama/nemotron-3-super:cloud` runs, the config should have `agents.defaults.model.primary` set to that value, and the file gets written correctly since `config get` confirms it. But after the gateway restarts, the new Telegram session is still reporting the old default `openai-codex/gpt-5.4` instead of the new one. So the issue is that `resolveDefaultModelForAgent` is returning the wrong value after restart—it's picking up the old default instead of using what was just configured.

The fact that the actual session model is `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6` tells me the gateway is actually using a different resolution path for the session model than for the default label in the reset message. The session model is resolving to `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6` correctly, but the default label is coming from somewhere else—likely the fallback path in `resolveConfiguredModelRef` that returns `{ provider: params.defaultProvider, model: params.defaultModel }`. This suggests the `rawModel` is empty when `resolveDefaultModelForAgent` tries to resolve it, which would cause it to skip the normal resolution and hit the fallback instead. I'm realizing the actual session model and the default model being displayed are two separate things. The session is using `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6` as intended, but the reset message is incorrectly showing `openai-codex/gpt-5.4` as the default—this is purely a display bug in the message, not an actual model selection problem. Looking at the system prompt more carefully, it's saying "default's set to GPT-5.4" which contradicts what the config actually says. This suggests either the config wasn't updated properly after the `models set` command, or there's a separate system prompt template that still references the old default. The gateway restart should have loaded the new config, but the message still references GPT-5.4 as the default even though the session model is set to `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6`.

Looking at the Telegram transcript, the bot responds twice to the `/new` command—once with the session confirmation message and once with the greeting and model note. The issue is that this model note appears in the second response, not the first, so it might be coming from a different part of the system that reads the default model from somewhere other than the config.

Since the issue was filed as a regression, something changed recently that broke this. I need to look at what commits touched the `models set` command, the `get-reply.ts` file, or the `resolveDefaultModelForAgent` function to find what caused this regression.

Looking at the git history, I see `a4c373935f` added `resolveAgentEffectiveModelPrimary` which now falls back to the global default when an agent has no model configured. Before this change, `resolveDefaultModelForAgent` would have taken a different path without this fallback logic, so the behavior of default model resolution must have shifted.

